<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zygii</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zichoi, Zygoi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zygii, or Zygoi, were a people of the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast mentioned by Greek and Roman geographers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, who described them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>. Generally seen as ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a>, they gave their name to the medieval land of Zichia, where Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread and Genoese merchants later kept trading posts. In Byzantine and Italian sources the Circassians long continued to be called Zichians.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zygii.jpg" alt="Zygii" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Professor Gustav Droysen (Hg.), Professor G. Hertzberg (Zeichnung) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Droysen_-_Ostr%C3%B6misches_Reich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
